I’ll first inspect the specimen metadata, pattern range, frozen index, and the supplied GSAS-II workflow notes. Then I’ll build a replayable multiphase checkpoint, validate residual families and phase fractions, and only run the prescribed staged polish after the phase inventory is settled.

The chemistry points first to ZnWO₄, with ZnO and/or WO₃ as plausible residual precursors, but I’m treating that only as a hypothesis. I’m now narrowing the frozen reference index by exact element system and comparing those candidate structures against the observed peak families before committing to an inventory.

The peak positions now give broad-family support for wolframite-type ZnWO₄: the low-angle 010/100/011/110 sequence and the split 111 pair all recur with one common specimen shift. I’m building that model by repairing the frozen NiWO₄ analogue’s asymmetric unit and substituting Zn, while keeping scheelite ZnWO₄, ZnO, and the available WO₃ polymorphs as explicit tests rather than assumptions.

The first Rietveld checkpoint confirms the wolframite topology but also exposed an over-parameterized calibration branch: simultaneous zero, specimen shift, cell and profile refinement drove zero/shift to bounds and made profile terms nonphysical. I’m discarding that branch, retaining the earlier physical cell/shift checkpoint, and tightening the refinement sequence and bounds before using residuals to judge extra phases.

The WO₃ family is unambiguous and drops Rwp materially. ZnO has chemically coherent candidate lines, but the unconstrained three-phase branch enlarged its basal cell by ~2.8% and broadened it to both profile limits—evidence that it was borrowing intensity from ZnWO₄, not yet an acceptable phase. I’m repeating that test with the reference-mismatch-sized cell window; ZnO will be retained only if its scale remains positive and its own line family is supported without that distortion.

The constrained three-phase branch is chemically self-consistent: ZnWO₄/WO₃/ZnO refines to about 85.4/10.8/3.7 wt%, and the residual-precursor ratio (WO₃:ZnO ≈ 2.90 by mass) matches the 1:1 molar feed’s stoichiometric ratio (2.85). I’m now discriminating the exact WO₃ polymorphs from the frozen index; after that I’ll lock the inventory and refine only supported profile/specimen terms before the one final staged coordinate polish.

The first bounded structural pass confirms the intensity problem is mainly the ZnWO₄ analogue coordinates: refining only Zn/W lowers Rwp from 19.8% to 15.7%, but Zn and several O components reach the ±0.10 Å adaptation window while bond geometry remains sound. That window is appropriate for final polishing but too narrow for the initial Ni→Zn analogue transfer, so I’m stopping this exploratory branch and restarting the ordinary adaptation with a modest ±0.20 Å window for ZnWO₄ only; exact-composition WO₃ and ZnO remain at ±0.10 Å.

The WO₃ reference comparison found an important source-quality issue: COD 1528915 matches peak positions but contains a 1.57 Å O–O contact. The nearly profile-equivalent COD 2311041 model has ordinary WO₆ geometry (minimum O–O 2.54 Å) and a slightly better checkpoint Rwp. Following the physical-model rule, I’m replacing 1528915 with a conventional-cell transformation of 2311041 before any further coordinate work.

The wider ZnWO₄ branch improved Rwp to 14.5%, but two coordinates again pinned at the new ±0.20 Å limits. That is not a trustworthy ZnWO₄ refinement; it signals the 1951 NiWO₄ parent is too crude and the coordinates are compensating intensity-systematics. I’m rejecting that branch and checking the frozen index’s later wolframite relatives for a better physical parent before proceeding.

The later wolframite parents resolve the clipping issue. The CoMoO₄-II transfer is the best checkpoint (Rwp 24.58% before profile terms versus 24.95% for the crude NiWO₄ model) and starts with ordinary Zn–O/W–O geometry; the W-bearing hubnerite transfer is profile-equivalent but slightly worse. I’m using CoMoO₄-II only as the coordinate parent, with Zn/W substitutions and a narrow bounded adaptation—the accepted topology remains wolframite P2/c ZnWO₄.

The phase inventory is now chemically and diffraction-wise coherent: ZnWO₄ with residual monoclinic WO₃ and wurtzite ZnO. I’m finishing the best physical checkpoint using the improved wolframite coordinate parent, then I’ll run the required one-time staged polish and export everything from the exact accepted project.

The improved checkpoint reaches Rwp 18.09% before structural intensity refinement, with 86.3/10.2/3.5 wt% ZnWO₄/WO₃/ZnO. I’m deliberately starting from the pre-polarization checkpoint because the earlier polarization trial pinned at its bound; coordinates should be adapted before deciding whether that correction is supported.

The principal ZnWO₄ coordinate adjustment gives a large, physical improvement (Rwp 16.26%, sensible Zn–O/W–O/O–O distances), but the minor WO₃ and ZnO coordinates immediately run to their bounds and do not improve the fit. That means their published geometries are supported as phase identities, while specimen-specific coordinate refinements are not reliable; I’ll retain those reference coordinates instead of overfitting them.

I’m also checking the frozen library’s framework-neighbour map before locking the principal model. This is a focused cross-chemistry check for a closer wolframite parent; it won’t reopen the already closed three-phase inventory.

A strong, phase-specific signal has emerged: only ZnWO₄ March–Dollase [111] texture gives a major improvement (Rwp 18.33→13.26%); all other principal axes refine essentially to 1. This is broad reflection-family evidence for real [111] texture, though its ratio is temporarily at the allowed edge because coordinates and the other intensity terms are not yet co-refined.

The [111] texture is unequivocal, but polarization and surface roughness are currently interchangeable intensity corrections: either lowers Rwp to about 11.88%, while using both adds no benefit and becomes unstable. I’m now refining only the well-supported principal-phase coordinates under each alternative; that should separate a real correction from one merely compensating for the starting analogue.

The coordinate-adjusted polarization branch is marginally better than the roughness branch and preserves more ordinary Zn–O/W–O geometry. I’m retaining polarization (an unavoidable instrument parameter) and dropping unsupported roughness. One remaining check is the phase Uiso scale, which can otherwise masquerade as polarization in the high-angle intensity falloff.

After the Uiso check, polarization settles cleanly at 0.95 and the phase fractions stabilize near 84.2/11.3/4.5 wt%. Two minor-phase texture signals are also reproducible and interior—WO₃ [100] and ZnO [001]—so I’m testing them jointly; bound-seeking alternatives are being rejected.

Every remaining coherent residual peak is centered on reflections of the accepted three phases; there is no separate unindexed family. Uniaxial-strain and roughness branches either became numerically singular, required out-of-range geometry, or worsened when fixed at physical bounds, so they are excluded. The complete physical checkpoint is now ready, and I’m invoking the required staged coordinate polish exactly once.

The one-time polish completed successfully and selected its “all” branch: Rwp improved from 10.9785% to 10.9519%, with clipped sites rolled back and the retained interior coordinates converged (maximum shift/esd 0.26). I’m auditing the branch geometry/fractions now before promoting it and exporting the submission files.

Completed the phase analysis and refinement.

- ZnWO₄, wolframite P2/c: 84.08 wt%
- WO₃, monoclinic P2₁/c: 11.43 wt%
- ZnO, wurtzite P6₃mc: 4.49 wt%
- Final fit: Rwp 10.952%, Rp 7.219%, GOF 4.144

The final project replayed successfully with no physical-profile flags. ZnO and WO₃ fractions have greater systematic uncertainty due to overlap and texture.

